Venus (Vulkan) on a Windows host -- WINQ-EMU patch series
==========================================================

What this is
------------

42 patches lifted from cmspam's virglrenderer fork, which is the only known
working implementation of Venus -- the virtio-gpu Vulkan protocol -- with a
Windows host.

    https://github.com/cmspam/winq-emu-virglrenderer   (branch alpha10)
    https://github.com/cmspam/winq-emu                 (the QEMU side)

Their fork is based on virglrenderer 1.3.0, which is exactly the version this
tree vendors, so the series applies to our pristine tarball with no rebasing.

Extracted 2026-08-10. The fork carries 64 commits on top of upstream 1.3.0;
the 22 that touch only the video-decode paths (DXVA/VA-API, which this project
does not use) were dropped. These 42 are the Venus and core commits.

Status: **all 42 apply cleanly to the pristine 1.3.0 tarball** (verified with
`git apply` against a fresh extraction). They are NOT applied to
download/virglrenderer-1.3.0, and Venus is NOT built -- our build still passes
-Dvenus=false. This is groundwork, not a live feature.

Why it matters
--------------

Venus forwards Vulkan rather than translating GL. That removes both of the
translation costs the GL path carries:

  * Shaders go across as SPIR-V, which is what Vulkan consumes natively. No
    NIR->TGSI downgrade and no GLSL regeneration on the host. (Mesa's virgl
    driver lowers NIR to TGSI purely to satisfy a wire format frozen in ~2014,
    before NIR existed; virglrenderer then reconstitutes GLSL *text* from it.)
  * Vulkan state is explicit in command buffers, so there is no GL state
    machine to rebuild host-side.

It is also the road to Proton and DXVK, i.e. actual Windows games in the guest.

What the series fixes for us specifically
-----------------------------------------

Building virglrenderer with -Dvenus=true under our mingw cross toolchain fails
on plain POSIX assumptions in the Venus code:

    src/venus/vkr_context.c:8   fatal error: sys/mman.h: No such file
    src/venus/vkr_device_memory.c:328  implicit declaration of getpagesize

Commit `30494489 venus+vrend: Windows dma-buf shim checkpoint` adds
src/venus/vkr_win32_shim.c and is what removes those. With the series applied,
both errors are gone and the build proceeds until it wants Vulkan headers --
which this build host simply does not have installed. That is a dependency to
add (Vulkan-Headers is header-only), not a porting problem.

Note their toolchain is MSYS2 UCRT64 while ours is mingw with msvcrt-os, kept
deliberately so binaries load on XP. The series compiling this far under our
toolchain is evidence that UCRT was their convenience rather than a Venus
requirement -- but that is not yet proven end to end.

What is still ours to build
---------------------------

The memory model, and it is the real work. Vulkan's contract is that the
application writes into memory the GPU reads, so host GPU-visible memory has to
appear in the guest's physical address space. WINQ-EMU solves this by pinning a
4 GiB region with Windows 11's `WHvAdviseGpaRange(Pin)`, a Hypervisor Platform
API -- they run under WHPX and do not own the guest's page tables.

We do own them, so that API is neither available nor needed. The direct
equivalent here: map the host-visible Vulkan allocation into cogpu-daemon,
lock it with an MDL, take the PFN array, and inject those machine frames into a
reserved region of the guest's p2m. The p2m indirection that FragRAM already
depends on is the mechanism -- guest PFN to machine frame is exactly the
translation being asked for. `virgl_renderer_resource_map_fixed` is the
host-side entry point their patches touch and where cogpu would hook in.

Also outstanding: the guest needs Mesa's Venus Vulkan driver built (we ship
Mesa 26.1.6; they require 24.0+), and the guest kernel needs virtio-gpu blob
resource support.

Validating it needs a modern Vulkan GPU. The GT 730 test box is Kepler on
NVIDIA's legacy 475 branch and cannot serve.
